6,"opponent":"CHI","home":true,"pf":9.0,"pa":14.0,"off_snaps":54,"epa_per_play":-0.09807,"pass_rate":0.7222,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6875,"def_epa_per_play":0.07762},{"week":17,"opponent":"LA","home":false,"pf":32.0,"pa":48.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":-0.05128,"pass_rate":0.6379,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4286,"def_epa_per_play":0.1404}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2018,"team":"NE","distance":0.8127,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Josh McDaniels"},{"season":2020,"team":"MIN","distance":0.8892,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"MIN","distance":0.9392,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Klint Kubiak"},{"season":2024,"team":"MIA","distance":1.077,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Mike McDaniel"},{"season":2025,"team":"SEA","distance":1.118,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Klint Kubiak"}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"IND","distance":0.5644,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"DAL","distance":0.6351,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"SEA","distance":0.6381,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6449,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"DAL","distance":0.6687,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The 49ers went 4-12 in 2018 and were outscored 435 to 342. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 24th in EPA per play and the defense 25th in EPA allowed. What set them apart was the highest 21 personnel usage and the 3rd highest Cover 3 rate in the league.","offense":"The 49ers offense was well below average by EPA per play. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the highest 21 personnel usage and the highest I formation usage in the league. Nick Mullens ran the passing game and leaned heavily on George Kittle, who drew 26% of the targets.","defense":"The 49ers defense was well below average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 11th and 29th. DeForest Buckner led the team with 12 sacks, and Cassius Marsh added 5.5 despite playing only 51% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the 3rd highest Cover 3 rate in the league.","special":"The 49ers were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 97% of field goals, which was the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 8% of the money on the books. Their secondary was among the youngest in the league.","players":"Nick Mullens played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,277 yards. George Kittle was the clear top target with 26% of the looks and 1,377 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Matt Breida and Alfred Morris. On defense, DeForest Buckner led the team with 12 sacks.","coaching":"Kyle Shanahan called the offense as head coach and Robert Saleh called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2018 Patriots under Josh McDaniels and the 2020 Vikings. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Colts and the 2019 Cowboys.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the 49ers changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}